diff --git a/docs/bench-cfgs/t1b-crc16.cfg b/docs/bench-cfgs/t1b-crc16.cfg
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ee8ddf4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bench-cfgs/t1b-crc16.cfg
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# TRIP 1b - is KOS's CRC16 loop worth replacing?  (about 30 seconds, no disc needed)
+# The SD driver computes a CRC16 over every 512-byte block it writes. Measured in
+# Trip 1, that loop is about 21 cycles a byte, roughly 13% of an SD write. This times
+# it against three table-driven versions on the same data, in 512-byte blocks each from
+# state 0 (what the driver does), and checks that all of them return identical values.
+#
+# Read the `BENCH hash ... crc16-*` lines: cyc_b is cycles per byte.
+# Predicted from instruction counts alone (NOT measured; the run decides):
+#   crc16-kos    28 instructions/byte  -> the ~21 cyc_b already seen
+#   crc16-table  16 instructions/byte  -> about 12
+#   crc16-slice2 ~11.5 instructions/byte -> about 9
+#   crc16-nibble more than KOS's        -> slower; it is here to be ruled out
+# `BENCH hash ... crc16 agree=OK` must appear. If it says MISMATCH, ignore the timings.
+#
+# What it is worth: a table CRC16 that saves S cycles a byte speeds an SD write by
+# S / 165 (165 cycles a byte at ui_hz=0), and an SD read by S / 289. The catch is
+# that it means patching KOS's sd.c (see docs/experiment-plan.md, item 6), so it is
+# only worth doing if the gain is several percent.
+
+ui_hz=0
+sections=hash
+hash_mib=8
+
+note=T1b CRC16 microbenchmark. card=<model>
diff --git a/docs/bench-cfgs/t3b-sd-crc.cfg b/docs/bench-cfgs/t3b-sd-crc.cfg
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..31e121d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bench-cfgs/t3b-sd-crc.cfg
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# TRIP 3b - what does the SD read CRC check cost?  (about 2 minutes, no disc needed)
+# check_crc (sd_crc) governs only the READ side: writes always compute and send a CRC16
+# and the card verifies it. So the read-rate difference between on and off is the cost of
+# KOS's CRC16 function alone, with the UI quiet. Run it twice.
+#
+# Measured 2026-09-19 (docs/evidence/sd-crc-read-cost-2026-09-19.json): reads 677.0 KiB/s
+# with the check, 733.5 without (+8.3%), writes unchanged, which puts KOS's loop at
+# 22.2 cycles a byte: 13.4% of an SD write. That is why Trip 1b looks at a faster function
+# rather than at turning the check off (it is the read-back's only wire-level protection).
+
+ui_hz=0
+sections=sd
+
+chunks=128
+sd_mib=8
+expand=off
+sd_if=scif
+sd_crc=on,off
+
+note=T3b SD read CRC on/off. card=<model>
diff --git a/docs/bench-cfgs/t6a-dma-probe.cfg b/docs/bench-cfgs/t6a-dma-probe.cfg
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..30751df
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bench-cfgs/t6a-dma-probe.cfg
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# TRIP 6a - does GD-ROM DMA work here, and what does a second thread see?  (about 2 minutes)
+# EXPERIMENTAL. Use a disc you can afford to be careful with and expect to reboot
+# afterwards if anything below says FAILED. Nothing here writes to the card or the disc.
+#
+# What it answers:
+#   1. Can the drive DMA raw 2352-byte sectors, and are the bytes identical to PIO?
+#      -> `BENCH dma check ... result=OK|MISMATCH|FAILED`, then a CRC gate per read size.
+#   2. How fast is DMA against PIO at the same size?     -> rd= on mode=dma vs plain lines.
+#   3. How much CPU does a read leave a second thread?   -> free= (100% = it lost nothing).
+#   4. Does a competing thread slow a PIO read?          -> rd= of the PIO lines with free=
+#      against the same PIO line without it. (This is what an SD-writing thread would do
+#      to the drive today, so it decides whether a plain thread split is viable.)
+#
+# Reading it (docs/experiment-plan.md, Trip 6):
+#   mode=dma free ~99, rd >= PIO           DMA hands the CPU back for free: the ideal overlap
+#   PIO free~40-60 and rd unchanged        a plain thread works; no DMA risk needed
+#   PIO rd drops sharply with a competitor PIO needs the CPU promptly; DMA is the way
+#   dma check FAILED                       DMA never completed by polling. Reboot, and the
+#                                          next step is an interrupt-driven variant
+# DMA timing includes up to one 10 ms scheduler tick of completion-detection latency
+# (about 8% at chunk 32, 2% at chunk 128); compare the two DMA lines at chunk 128 first.
+
+ui_hz=1
+sections=sweep
+
+sweep_fads=45150
+sweep_chunks=32,128
+sweep_mode=pio,dma
+sweep_spin=off,on
+sweep_gap_us=0
+sweep_service_us=0
+sweep_sectors=2048
+sweep_verify=on
+
+yield_us=2000
+note=T6a DMA probe. card=<model> disc=<title>
diff --git a/docs/bench-cfgs/t7-real-capture-fast.cfg b/docs/bench-cfgs/t7-real-capture-fast.cfg
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..82dafc7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bench-cfgs/t7-real-capture-fast.cfg
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# TRIP 7 - one REAL, complete capture with the fast settings, then prove it on the PC.
+# (about 40-60 minutes of capture, plus a few minutes on the PC)
+#
+# The bench measures 4096-sector slices of the capture engine. This checks the whole thing:
+# a full disc, ripped with the settings the bench says are fastest, must come out
+# BYTE-IDENTICAL to a known-good dump. Nothing else proves that.
+#
+# Use the Sword of the Berserk disc: its correct CRC32s are already known
+# (docs/evidence/sword-of-the-berserk-reference.json), so the PC check has an answer key.
+# Do NOT press X (resume): a resumed job keeps the hash mode it started with, so it would
+# ignore these settings. Start a NEW dump (A on the Capture page) and let it finish.
+#
+# What to send back: the auto-saved report, the time it took, and the output of
+#   python3 tools/verify_dump.py <the new folder under KUI/dumps> \
+#       --reference docs/evidence/sword-of-the-berserk-reference.json
+
+ui_hz=2
+capture_hash=crc32
+end_readback=off
+
+note=T7 real capture, fast settings. card=<model> disc=Sword of the Berserk
